1
00:00:00,353 --> 00:00:04,037
Someone asked your AI trip
planner for a city stay for four.

2
00:00:04,087 --> 00:00:06,465
It suggests a studio for two.

3
00:00:06,505 --> 00:00:10,588
Cozy, potentially friendship ending.

4
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:08,770
[dramatic sting]

5
00:00:10,753 --> 00:00:15,603
The request completed successfully though,
and there's no error in sight.

6
00:00:15,753 --> 00:00:17,703
So what went wrong?

7
00:00:18,087 --> 00:00:22,147
In this lesson, we'll use a trace
in PostHog's AI observability

8
00:00:22,187 --> 00:00:24,047
to see inside one request.

9
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:28,170
We'll look at every model call,
every tool, and every message.

10
00:00:28,453 --> 00:00:31,380
We'll work backwards from
the answer, find the bug,

11
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:34,503
then check that the fix actually works.

12
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:38,698
Say your app is Twig, a rental
site with an AI trip planner.

13
00:00:38,738 --> 00:00:41,488
Here's roughly what happens
when someone uses it.

14
00:00:41,587 --> 00:00:44,470
Someone types a message
and it goes to the model.

15
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:49,303
The model figures it needs to look
something up, so it calls Twig's search.

16
00:00:49,353 --> 00:00:52,203
The search sends back a place to stay.

17
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:54,970
And the model turns that into a reply.

18
00:00:55,087 --> 00:00:57,880
That reply goes back to
the person who asked,

19
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,913
and as far as anyone can tell, it worked.

20
00:01:00,953 --> 00:01:04,732
But the visitor asked for four,
and this place sleeps two.

21
00:01:04,772 --> 00:01:07,922
Somewhere in those four steps,
something went wrong.

22
00:01:07,987 --> 00:01:12,147
To debug this, we start with the one
thing we know is wrong, the answer.

23
00:01:12,187 --> 00:01:15,213
Then we ask, what did the model receive?

24
00:01:15,253 --> 00:01:17,303
Where did that information come from?

25
00:01:17,353 --> 00:01:19,170
And what inputs produced it?

26
00:01:19,453 --> 00:01:21,613
The answer on its own can't tell us.

27
00:01:21,653 --> 00:01:24,137
We need to see inside the request.

28
00:01:24,287 --> 00:01:26,537
So how does PostHog see this?

29
00:01:26,987 --> 00:01:30,647
Each model call goes through
the PostHog SDK with a trace ID,

30
00:01:30,687 --> 00:01:32,770
and the search goes as a span.

31
00:01:32,887 --> 00:01:36,103
All of it lands in PostHog as one trace.

32
00:01:36,287 --> 00:01:40,813
A trace is everything your app recorded
for one request, one message in,

33
00:01:40,853 --> 00:01:43,703
one answer out, and every step in between.

34
00:01:43,787 --> 00:01:48,598
The model call is a generation,
the messages we sent and what came back.

35
00:01:48,638 --> 00:01:51,913
Here, it asks for a tool, search_stays.

36
00:01:51,953 --> 00:01:55,398
Our app runs the search
and records it as a span.

37
00:01:55,438 --> 00:01:57,165
A span is one step of work

38
00:01:57,205 --> 00:02:00,222
with the filters going in
and the results coming back.

39
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:02,947
The results go to a second generation.

40
00:02:02,987 --> 00:02:05,337
That's where Twig's answer comes from.

41
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:07,865
All three share one trace ID.

42
00:02:07,905 --> 00:02:09,922
That's how PostHog groups them.

43
00:02:10,153 --> 00:02:12,903
Let's start with what the model received.

44
00:02:12,953 --> 00:02:16,003
The visitor asked for
a city stay for four,

45
00:02:16,087 --> 00:02:19,970
and the search returned
Le Nid Chic, capacity

46
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:23,370
[romantic accordion music]

47
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:24,613
2.

48
00:02:24,653 --> 00:02:28,498
Hmm, so the model was handed a
stay that can't fit the group

49
00:02:28,538 --> 00:02:31,598
and its instructions say to
recommend the first result.

50
00:02:31,638 --> 00:02:33,588
So the model did its job.

51
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:35,270
Let's open the search.

52
00:02:35,553 --> 00:02:38,513
Ah, look here, it filtered for city stays.

53
00:02:38,553 --> 00:02:42,047
Good. But there's no guest filter at all.

54
00:02:42,087 --> 00:02:44,198
Who picked those filters?

55
00:02:44,238 --> 00:02:47,088
Well, it was the model
in the first generation.

56
00:02:47,187 --> 00:02:49,847
See here, it couldn't ask for guests.

57
00:02:49,887 --> 00:02:52,047
The tool only takes a setting.

58
00:02:52,087 --> 00:02:55,970
So the search worked, it just
searched for the wrong thing.

59
00:02:56,287 --> 00:02:58,513
So where is that tool defined?

60
00:02:58,553 --> 00:03:00,470
We could dig through our code.

61
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:04,703
I mean, let's just send a
robot to dig through our code.

62
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:07,803
Look, the robot found it.

63
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:12,613
The search_stays tool has a setting,
but nowhere for the group size.

64
00:03:12,653 --> 00:03:15,147
So we have the robot
add a guests parameter,

65
00:03:15,187 --> 00:03:17,937
so the search only returns stays that fit.

66
00:03:18,053 --> 00:03:24,022
Then we rerun the same request with the
same model and instructions to test it.

67
00:03:23,670 --> 00:03:25,270
[cheering]

68
00:03:26,053 --> 00:03:29,780
In the new trace,
the model asks for four guests,

69
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:33,803
and the search returns
The Coop, capacity 4.

70
00:03:34,053 --> 00:03:36,937
The model got a stay that
fits and recommended it.

71
00:03:37,053 --> 00:03:38,170
Good robot.

72
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,000
[cute robot beeps]

73
00:03:39,553 --> 00:03:42,713
The first call succeeded,
so nothing alerted us.

74
00:03:42,753 --> 00:03:47,170
We'd have found out when
the lawsuit arrived.

75
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:47,700
[gavel bangs]

76
00:03:47,287 --> 00:03:50,580
Success means the model ran,
not that it was right.

77
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:54,880
Next time your AI feature gets it wrong,
ask the same three questions.

78
00:03:54,920 --> 00:03:56,513
What did the model receive?

79
00:03:56,553 --> 00:03:58,480
Where did that information come from?

80
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:00,470
And what inputs produced it?

81
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:03,837
Then rerun the request
and follow the evidence.

82
00:04:03,887 --> 00:04:08,513
Now this process works for a
dozen traces, but as Twig grows,

83
00:04:08,553 --> 00:04:11,598
there are more traces
than any human can read.

84
00:04:11,638 --> 00:04:14,183
So how do you know it's
getting answers right?

85
00:04:14,223 --> 00:04:17,258
That's where evals come in,
in the next video.

86
00:04:17,387 --> 00:04:23,337
To learn more about PostHog's AI
observability, visit posthog.com/learn.
